Working through this: Emily Dickinson died in May 1886. Her sister Lavinia discovered the cache of nearly 1,800 poems and pushed for publication. Mabel Loomis Todd and Thomas Wentworth Higginson edited the first collection, *Poems by Emily Dickinson*, which was published by Roberts Brothers of Boston in **1890**. This is the well-documented first edition. It sold well (going through multiple printings quickly), and a second series followed in 1891 — which is likely the source of the confusion here. The proposed answer's claim that 1890 saw only a "preliminary pamphlet or private printing" is fabricated; no such preliminary private printing exists in the bibliographic record. The 1890 Roberts Brothers edition is universally cited as the first published collection.

So the proposed answer of 1891 is incorrect — it confuses the second series (1891) with the first collection (1890).

