The 1902 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was awarded to **Ronald Ross**, not Giovanni Battista Grassi. Ross, a British army surgeon working in India, demonstrated in 1897–1898 that mosquitoes transmit malaria parasites, specifically showing the parasite's development in mosquitoes and transmission to birds. He received the Nobel Prize in 1902 "for his work on malaria, by which he has shown how it enters the organism and thereby has laid the foundation for successful research on this disease and methods of combating it."

Grassi did make important contributions—he worked out the human malaria cycle via *Anopheles* and conducted the hut experiment described—but the Nobel Committee chose Ross. Notably, the Nobel archives show the committee seriously considered Grassi but ultimately rejected him, partly due to a fierce priority dispute with Ross. The proposed answer is incorrect.

