The proposed answer is incorrect. When Alfred Marshall retired in 1908, he was succeeded as Professor of Political Economy at Cambridge by **Arthur Cecil Pigou**, his student and colleague. Pigou held the chair from 1908 until 1943.

John Maynard Keynes never held this chair. In 1908 Keynes was only 25 and working in the India Office (returning to Cambridge later that year as a fellow of King's College, but not as a professor). Keynes' celebrated academic career at Cambridge was based on his fellowship and later his involvement in economics through books and government work — he never occupied the Chair of Political Economy. The claim that Keynes "established himself through his work on probability theory" by 1908 is also wrong, since *A Treatise on Probability* was published in 1921.

